/-!
# Stacks 0F9D for arbitrary finite locally free modules, and the cap-family Chern character

Let `(S, δ)` be the base situation of chapter 42 of the Stacks Project, Stacks 02QL, let `X` be
locally of finite type over `S`, let `ℰ₁` and `ℰ₂` be finite locally free `𝒪_X`-modules of ranks
`r` and `s`, and let `P_p` be the universal power-sum polynomial of Stacks 0F9B. This file proves
both conclusions of [Stacks, Tag 0F9D](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F9D) in the ring of
graded cap families: the source's more precise identity

  `P_p(ℰ₁ ⊗_{𝒪_X} ℰ₂) = ∑_{p₁ + p₂ = p} C(p, p₁) P_{p₁}(ℰ₁) P_{p₂}(ℰ₂)`,

and the headline identity

  `ch(ℰ₁ ⊗_{𝒪_X} ℰ₂) = ch(ℰ₁) ch(ℰ₂)`,

which is the first divided by `p!`.

**Stacks 0F9D itself is proved elsewhere, in the bivariant ring and without the constant-rank
hypothesis**, by `Stacks.Chow.modulePowerSumArbitraryRank_tensorObj` and
`Stacks.Chow.modulePowerSumComponentArbitraryRank_tensorObj` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.ChernClassArbitraryRank.BivariantTensorPowerSum`, and by
`Stacks.Chow.totalChernCharacterArbitraryRank_tensorObj` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.ChernClassArbitraryRank.BivariantChernCharacter`. The results
of this file are the constant-rank step they rest on: the identity proved here, read on every
test scheme, is what makes the identity of bivariant classes.

The Chern character built here to hold `ch(ℰ₁ ⊗ ℰ₂) = ch(ℰ₁) ch(ℰ₂)` is also where the
constant-rank, cap-family form of the additivity

  `ch(ℰ) = ch(ℰ₁) + ch(ℰ₂)`  for a short exact sequence  `0 ⟶ ℰ₁ ⟶ ℰ ⟶ ℰ₂ ⟶ 0`

of [Stacks, Tag 0F9C](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F9C) is proved, in
`Stacks.Chow.totalRationalChernCharacter_eq_add_of_shortExact`. That one is the source's more
precise equality `P_p(ℰ) = P_p(ℰ₁) + P_p(ℰ₂)`, proved with no extra hypotheses in
`Stacks.Chow.GenuineChern.powerSumFamily_chernFamily_eq_add_of_shortExact`, divided by `p!`; the
splitting principle is not redone for it. Stacks 0F9C itself is registered where the source states
it, in the rational Chow cohomology `A^p(X) ⊗ 𝐐` and with no constancy of the ranks assumed, on
`Stacks.Chow.moduleChernCharacter_eq_add_of_shortExact` in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.ChernClassArbitraryRank.BivariantPowerSumShortExact`.

## How the statement is read

The constant-rank identity of Stacks 0F9D proved below is an identity in the ring
`Stacks.Chow.GradedCapFamily (Stacks.Chow.chowGroup C sX)` of graded cap families — the ring in
which the total Chern class of Stacks 02U6 lives,
`Stacks.Chow.GenuineChern.chernFamily`. `P_p(ℱ)` is
`Stacks.Chow.powerSumChernElem`: the value of `P_p` at the rank and the Chern classes of `ℱ`,
computed in the commutative subring the Chern classes generate.
`Stacks.Chow.powerSumChernElem_zero` and `Stacks.Chow.powerSumChernElem_one` identify it with the
rank and with `c₁(ℱ)` in the first two degrees.
`Stacks.Chow.powerSumChernElem_tensorObj_apply` reads the identity against a fixed cycle `α`, in
`A_{n-p}(X)`.

The bivariant ring `A^*(X) = ∏_{q ≥ 0} A^q(X)` does exist in this repository: it is
`Stacks.Chow.TotalChowClass`, with `Stacks.Chow.TotalChowClass.instRing`, whose product is the
Cauchy product of the compositions `A^i(X) × A^j(X) ⟶ A^{i+j}(X)` of Stacks 0B75. The cap-family
ring used here is its image under `Stacks.Chow.TotalChowClass.op`, the operation of a total class
on the Chow groups of a test scheme, which is a ring homomorphism
(`Stacks.Chow.TotalChowClass.opRingHom`).

### Where `ch(ℰ₁) ch(ℰ₂)` is formed

The source reads `ch` in `A^*(X) ⊗ 𝐐`, and that ring is available:
`Stacks.Chow.rationalTotalChowClass` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.ChernClassArbitraryRank.BivariantChernCharacter`, where
`Stacks.Chow.totalChernCharacterArbitraryRank_tensorObj` carries Stacks 0F9D. In *this* file `ch`
is formed instead in the ring where the more precise identity proved here already lives:
`Stacks.Chow.rationalChernSubring`, the commutative subring
`Stacks.Chow.chernSubring` of cap families generated by the Chern classes, tensored over `𝐙`
with `𝐐` so that `p!` becomes invertible. There `ch_p(ℱ) = P_p(ℱ)/p!` is
`Stacks.Chow.rationalChernCharacter` and the two Chern characters have a product in the same
ring, which is what the multiplicative identity of Stacks 0F9D needs. The additive identity of
Stacks 0F9C needs no product, and is registered in `A^p(X) ⊗ 𝐐` itself as recorded above.

The whole of `ch(ℱ) = (ch_0(ℱ), ch_1(ℱ), ch_2(ℱ), …)` is
`Stacks.Chow.totalRationalChernCharacter`, a formal power series over that ring. The variable is
bookkeeping for the grading only: `PowerSeries A` is the product `∏_{p ≥ 0} A` with the
convolution multiplication, and that product under that multiplication is the home Stacks 0ESX
gives the Chern character. An identity of two such series is therefore an identity of two Chern
characters, holding in every degree at once.

## The proof is the source's

The source chooses `π : P ⟶ X` as in Stacks 02UL, uses injectivity of `π^*` to reduce to modules
filtered by invertible modules, and computes with the Chern roots `x_i = c₁(ℒ_i)` and
`y_j = c₁(𝒩_j)`. Every step is discharged:

* **Stacks 02UL** is `Stacks.Chow.exists_splittingMorphism_unconditional`, applied to the pair
  `ℰ₁`, `ℰ₂`; `Stacks.Chow.exists_hasQuotients_of_hasInvertibleFiltration` names the successive
  quotients of the two filtrations it delivers;
* **the Chern roots of `π^*ℰ₁` and `π^*ℰ₂`** are the `c₁(ℒ_i)` and the `c₁(𝒩_j)` by
  `Stacks.Chow.hasChernRoots_of_hasQuotients`, which is Stacks 02UJ read coefficientwise;
* **the Chern roots of `π^*(ℰ₁ ⊗ ℰ₂)`** are the `r · s` sums `x_i + y_j` by
  `Stacks.Chow.hasChernRoots_tensorObj_of_hasQuotients`, transported along Stacks 01CD,
  `Stacks.Chow.pullbackTensorObjIso`, which identifies `π^*(ℰ₁ ⊗ ℰ₂)` with `π^*ℰ₁ ⊗ π^*ℰ₂`;
* **the universal algebra** — the binomial theorem for `(x_i + y_j)^p` followed by Fubini — is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.powerSumOfChernClasses_tensor_of_hasChernRoots`;
* **the descent along `π^*`** is `Stacks.Chow.eq_of_intertwines`, whose two inputs are Stacks 02U8
  for `P_p` (`Stacks.Chow.intertwines_powerSumChernElem`, from
  `Stacks.Chow.GenuineChern.intertwines_chernFamily`) and clause (1) of Stacks 02UL, the field
  `Stacks.Chow.SplittingMorphism.injective_pullbackChow`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Chow.powerSumChernSub` — `P_p(ℱ)` inside the commutative subring, before it is pushed
  into the ring of graded cap families.
* `Stacks.Chow.rationalChernSubring` — that subring tensored with `𝐐`, where `p!` is invertible.
* `Stacks.Chow.rationalChernCharacter`, `Stacks.Chow.totalRationalChernCharacter` — `ch_p(ℱ)` and
  the whole of `ch(ℱ)`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Chow.powerSumChernElem_tensorObj` — the source's more precise identity of Stacks
  0F9D, in the ring of graded cap families, for constant ranks; this is the step the bivariant
  form of the tag rests on.
* `Stacks.Chow.powerSumChernElem_tensorObj_apply` — the same identity read against a fixed cycle,
  in `A_{n-p}(X)`.
* `Stacks.Chow.totalRationalChernCharacter_tensorObj` — the headline identity
  `ch(ℰ₁ ⊗ ℰ₂) = ch(ℰ₁) ch(ℰ₂)` of Stacks 0F9D in the cap-operator ring, for constant ranks.
* `Stacks.Chow.rationalChernCharacter_tensorObj` — the headline identity in each degree,
  `ch_p(ℰ₁ ⊗ ℰ₂) = ∑_{p₁ + p₂ = p} ch_{p₁}(ℰ₁) ch_{p₂}(ℰ₂)`.
* `Stacks.Chow.totalRationalChernCharacter_eq_add_of_shortExact` — the constant-rank, cap-family
  form of `ch(ℰ) = ch(ℰ₁) + ch(ℰ₂)`.
* `Stacks.Chow.rationalChernCharacter_eq_add_of_shortExact` — that identity in each degree,
  `ch_p(ℰ) = ch_p(ℰ₁) + ch_p(ℰ₂)`.
* `Stacks.Chow.powerSumChernElem_eq_powerSumFamily_of_pos` — the two spellings of `P_p` agree in
  positive degrees, which is what carries the additivity into the ring where `ch` is formed.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F9D](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F9D)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F9C](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F9C)
